  "body": {
    "id": "prop:two-horizon-shared-cell-witness",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "For \\(\\mathcal W^{(2)}\\), the untreated mean has rank one, \\(\\mu_{2,0}=\\mu_{2,1}=1\\), \\(A_{2,0}=A_{2,1}=4\\), and \\(B_2=1\\). At each horizon, the four treated target cells have coefficient \\(1/4\\), the four donor target cells have coefficient \\(-1/4\\), and the four treated pre-period cells shared by both horizons have coefficient \\(-1/4\\). Therefore each marginal variance is \\(12(1/4)^2=3/4\\), each shared-cell covariance product is \\((-1/4)(-1/4)=1/16\\), the off-diagonal covariance is \\(4/16=1/4\\), and the correlation is \\((1/4)/(3/4)=1/3\\). This explicitly satisfies the noncancellation condition in \\(\\mathrm{lem:staircase\\mbox{-}covariance}\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "M0",
      "r",
      "mu",
      "A",
      "B",
      "c",
      "Omega",
      "Sigma"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:two-horizon-witness",
      "lem:staircase-covariance"
    ],
    "justification": "The fully enumerated finite panel verifies the coefficient normalization and exhibits a nonzero signed shared-cell sum without appealing to an unnamed procedure.",
    "gap": "No located low-rank staggered-adoption inference result displays this exact two-horizon coefficient calculation; the proposition is an algebraic witness, not a claim that a published method imposes block independence.",
    "consumer": "The twelve-cell calculation is a deterministic unit test for implementations of the full RTC-law event covariance.",
    "proof_tex": "In \\(\\mathcal W^{(2)}\\),\n\\[\nM^0=\\mathbf 1_8\\mathbf 1_3^{\\top},\n\\]\nso \\(\\operatorname{rank}(M^0)=1\\).  For either \\(k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\), the\neligible Choi--Yuan block is the \\(8\\)-by-\\(2\\) all-ones matrix.  One\ncompact singular value decomposition has\n\\[\nU_{2,k}=\\frac{\\mathbf 1_8}{\\sqrt 8},\\qquad\nV_{2,k}=\\frac{\\mathbf 1_2}{\\sqrt 2},\\qquad\n\\psi_{2,k}=\\sqrt{8\\cdot2}=4.\n\\]\nConsequently\n\\[\n\\mu_{2,k}\n=\\max\\left\\{8\\left\\|U_{2,k}\\right\\|_{2,\\infty}^2,\n2\\left\\|V_{2,k}\\right\\|_{2,\\infty}^2\\right\\}\n=\\max\\{8(1/8),2(1/2)\\}=1.\n\\]\nBecause every loading and factor equals one and the donor and pre-period\nsets have cardinalities four and one, respectively,\n\\[\nA_{2,k}=\\sum_{j=5}^8x_j^2=4,\n\\qquad\nB_2=\\sum_{s\\in S_2}z_s^2=z_1^2=1.\n\\]\nFor both horizons, \\(E_k=\\{2\\}\\), \\(N_k=n_2=4\\), and hence the outer\ncohort weight in the definition of \\(c_{kq}\\) is one.  Substitution into\nthat definition gives, with \\(t_k=2+k\\),\n\\[\nc_{k,(u,v)}=\n\\begin{cases}\n  1/4,&u\\in I_2,\\ v=t_k,\\\\\n -1/4,&u\\in J_{2,k},\\ v=t_k,\\\\\n -1/4,&u\\in I_2,\\ v=1,\\\\\n 0,&\\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nThe three nonzero cases are disjoint and each contains four cells.\nSince every cell variance is one, the definition of \\(\\Omega_n\\) yields\n\\[\n\\Omega_{kk}=4(1/4)^2+4(-1/4)^2+4(-1/4)^2=\\frac34.\n\\]\nThe treated target cells and donor target cells occur at calendar time\n\\(2+k\\), and therefore are distinct across \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).  The\nonly shared nonzero cells are the four cells \\((u,1)\\) with \\(u\\in I_2\\).\nEach contributes\n\\[\nc_{0,(u,1)}c_{1,(u,1)}=(-1/4)(-1/4)=\\frac1{16}.\n\\]\nApplying \\(\\texttt{lem:staircase-covariance}\\) therefore gives\n\\[\n\\Omega_{01}=4\\left(\\frac1{16}\\right)=\\frac14,\n\\qquad\n\\Sigma_{01}=\\frac{\\Omega_{01}}{\n\\sqrt{\\Omega_{00}\\Omega_{11}}}\n=\\frac{1/4}{3/4}=\\frac13.\n\\]\nThus the signed shared-cell sum is nonzero, as claimed.",
